---
import UniPage from '../components/UniPage.astro';
import { getUniversity } from '../data/universities';

const uni = getUniversity('vtu')!;
---

<UniPage
  uni={uni}
  title="VTU CGPA to Percentage Calculator (Official ×10 Formula) | GlobalCGPA"
  description="Convert VTU CGPA to percentage instantly using the official Visvesvaraya Technological University formula CGPA × 10. Free calculator with reverse conversion, conversion table, First Class thresholds and FAQ."
  intro="Enter your VTU CGPA to get your percentage instantly using the official ×10 conversion — plus reverse conversion, a full reference table and VTU class thresholds for placements and higher studies."
>
  <h2>How the VTU CGPA to Percentage Conversion Works</h2>
  <p>Visvesvaraya Technological University (VTU), based in Belagavi, affiliates most engineering colleges in Karnataka. Unlike universities that use offset-based formulas, VTU's official conversion is the simplest in India: your percentage is your CGPA multiplied by 10.</p>
  <div class="formula-block">
    <div class="f-label">VTU formula</div>
    <div class="f-text">Percentage = CGPA × 10</div>
  </div>
  <p>This rule applies across VTU's CBCS schemes (2015, 2017, 2018 and the 2021-22 scheme), so whether you are a current student or graduated a few years ago, the same multiplication applies. Students on very old marks-based schemes already have percentages on their marksheets and do not need any conversion.</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>CGPA</th><th>Calculation</th><th>VTU Percentage</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>9.5</td><td>9.5 × 10</td><td>95%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8.75</td><td>8.75 × 10</td><td>87.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8.0</td><td>8.0 × 10</td><td>80%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7.0</td><td>7.0 × 10</td><td>70%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6.2</td><td>6.2 × 10</td><td>62%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>VTU Class Thresholds: What Your Percentage Means</h2>
  <p>Because VTU uses the ×10 multiplier, the class boundaries line up neatly with round percentages. <strong>First Class with Distinction</strong> requires roughly 7.0 CGPA (70%), <strong>First Class</strong> starts at 6.0 CGPA (60%), and <strong>Second Class</strong> at 5.0 CGPA (50%). Distinction awards can carry an additional condition — such as clearing all subjects in a single attempt — depending on your scheme, so read the classification section of your own regulation if you are close to a boundary.</p>
  <p>These thresholds matter most during placement season. Many companies that visit VTU campuses filter applicants at 60% or 70%, which is exactly 6.0 or 7.0 CGPA under the VTU formula.</p>

  <h2>SGPA, CGPA and Your Final VTU Percentage</h2>
  <p>Your VTU marksheet shows an SGPA for each semester and a cumulative CGPA once multiple semesters are complete. The CGPA is a credit-weighted average, meaning semesters with more credits pull the final figure more strongly. If you want to project your final CGPA from your semester SGPAs before results are consolidated, use our <a href="/sgpa-to-cgpa">SGPA to CGPA calculator</a> — it applies exactly the credit-weighted formula VTU uses, then you can convert the result to a percentage here.</p>

  <h2>Using Your VTU Percentage Abroad</h2>
  <p>For MS applications in the US, Canada or Europe, most universities prefer to see your original CGPA with the scale explained (for example, "8.4/10.0, VTU India") rather than a self-converted percentage. Credential evaluators such as WES recalculate from your official transcripts directly. As a quick self-check, the linear approximation GPA ≈ (CGPA ÷ 10) × 4 puts an 8.0 CGPA near 3.2 on the 4.0 scale — our <a href="/cgpa-to-gpa">CGPA to GPA converter</a> handles the maths for you.</p>
  <p>For Indian purposes — GATE counselling, PSU forms, state government jobs in Karnataka, and campus placements — the ×10 percentage from this page is the figure to use.</p>
</UniPage>
